<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 20: Nuclear Binding Energy and Reactions</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> Reading covers sections 8.6 through 8.8. 
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Explain why the graph in page 238 shows that iron is more stable than both helium and uranium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
	        In atomic mass units, the masses of proton, neutron, and helium (2 protons and 2 neutrons) are, 
            respectively, <m>~{m_p=1.00728u},~{m_n=1.00866u},~{m_{He}=4.001506u}</m>. To answer the questions below,
            you will need the conversion factors <m>1\text{u}=931.45 \text{MeV}</m> and 
            <m>1 \text{MeV}=1.6\times10^{(-13)} \text{J}</m>.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                  How much energy does it take to break apart a helium nucleus? By this I mean the energy
                  to take the helium nucleus and turn it into 2 protons and 2 neutrons that are infinitely far
                  away from each other, with no kinetic energy. This is known as the <term>binding energy</term>
                  <m>B</m> of helium.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the ratio of the binding energy of helium to its rest energy?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The mass of the hydrogen atom is <m>m_H=1.00783</m>u, and its binding energy is 
                    <m>13.6</m> eV. What is the ratio of the binding energy of hydrogen to its rest energy?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Using your calculations from this question, explain why conservation of mass holds
                    in chemistry but not in nuclear physics.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>

      </statement>
    </exercise>


  </page>
</worksheet>